WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.382
Two regions keep copies of one key: the set of blocked cards.

2
00:00:07.382 --> 00:00:10.223
Both copies say card 2290 is blocked.

3
00:00:10.433 --> 00:00:18.450
At the same moment, client A blocks card 4417 through EU, and client B blocks card 5310 through US.

4
00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:21.284
Neither has seen the other's write.

5
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:23.468
Then the replicas swap versions.

6
00:00:23.468 --> 00:00:25.375
Which set should each one keep?

7
00:00:25.567 --> 00:00:30.876
Last write wins stamps every write with the clock and keeps the higher stamp.

8
00:00:30.876 --> 00:00:33.634
EU's write says 2 milliseconds past ten.

9
00:00:33.634 --> 00:00:34.461
US's says 1.

10
00:00:34.633 --> 00:00:37.005
So both replicas keep EU's set.

11
00:00:37.005 --> 00:00:41.900
Card 5310 is gone, though client B was told its block succeeded.

12
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:45.166
The clock can't tell what each writer had seen.

13
00:00:45.166 --> 00:00:46.535
A version vector can.

14
00:00:46.535 --> 00:00:52.406
Each version keeps one counter per replica: how many of that replica's writes it has seen.

15
00:00:52.406 --> 00:00:55.993
The starting set had seen two from EU and none from US.

16
00:00:56.167 --> 00:00:59.873
A write adds one to its own replica's counter.

17
00:00:59.873 --> 00:01:03.097
A's write through EU becomes EU 3, US 0.

18
00:01:03.097 --> 00:01:06.320
B's write through US becomes EU 2, US 1.

19
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:09.271
To compare two versions, line the counters up.

20
00:01:09.271 --> 00:01:16.199
If every counter in one is at least as big as in the other, its writer saw the other version, so it may replace it.

21
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:20.308
Here A's version is ahead on EU, and B's is ahead on US.

22
00:01:20.308 --> 00:01:23.030
Each holds a write the other never saw.

23
00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:24.356
They're concurrent.

24
00:01:24.533 --> 00:01:26.663
So neither may replace the other.

25
00:01:26.663 --> 00:01:31.117
Each replica keeps both, as siblings, and the next read returns both.

26
00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:39.925
Client A reads both, blocks all three cards, and writes back through EU with a vector that covers both: EU 4, US 1.

27
00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:46.262
It's at least as big as both siblings on every counter, so it replaces them on both replicas.

28
00:01:46.262 --> 00:01:47.388
No write is lost.

29
00:01:47.567 --> 00:01:50.504
One write may replace another only if it saw it.

30
00:01:50.504 --> 00:01:56.135
The version vector says whether it did, and when neither saw the other, keep both and merge.
